WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.440
ShellCheck fundamentals are best
understood as a repeatable review habit.

2
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.990
Run ShellCheck early and repeatedly.

3
00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.781
Read each finding in context.

4
00:00:13.861 --> 00:00:20.476
Understand the shell behavior behind the
message before changing code.

5
00:00:20.556 --> 00:00:25.327
Fix root causes rather than only quieting
warnings.

6
00:00:25.407 --> 00:00:29.094
When a finding points to unclear intent,

7
00:00:29.174 --> 00:00:36.006
use it as a chance to improve the script's
design, documentation, or tests.

8
00:00:36.086 --> 00:00:42.592
Pay close attention to quoting, word
splitting, globbing, variables,

9
00:00:42.672 --> 00:00:48.962
conditionals, command substitutions,
pipelines, and exit status.

10
00:00:49.042 --> 00:00:56.632
These are the areas where shell scripts
often behave differently from what authors

11
00:00:56.712 --> 00:00:57.471
expect.

12
00:00:57.551 --> 00:01:03.407
Preserving argument boundaries, checking
important results,

13
00:01:03.487 --> 00:01:11.186
and making control flow explicit can
prevent many operational failures before they

14
00:01:11.266 --> 00:01:13.001
reach production.

15
00:01:13.081 --> 00:01:17.310
Match the script to its intended
interpreter.

16
00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:24.656
The shebang, shell syntax, and runtime
environment should tell the same story.

17
00:01:24.736 --> 00:01:29.941
If a script uses Bash features, declare
and run it as Bash.

18
00:01:30.021 --> 00:01:35.660
If it must be portable, write and test it
for that requirement.

19
00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.342
Portability is not a slogan;

20
00:01:38.422 --> 00:01:45.145
it is an engineering decision that should
match where the script will run.

21
00:01:45.225 --> 00:01:52.491
Use suppressions sparingly, document them
clearly, and review them over time.

22
00:01:52.571 --> 00:01:58.752
Integrate ShellCheck into editor
workflows, pull request review,

23
00:01:58.832 --> 00:02:05.555
and CI/CD so script quality improves
before merge and before deployment.

24
00:02:05.635 --> 00:02:11.382
ShellCheck is not a substitute for
judgment, runtime testing,

25
00:02:11.462 --> 00:02:18.836
or operational validation, but it is one
of the easiest ways to raise the baseline

26
00:02:18.916 --> 00:02:22.820
quality of shell automation across a team.
